<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Chan Santa Cruz</h1><p class="st-meta">1850 CE – 1901 CE · capital Chan Santa Cruz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Independent Maya state in eastern Yucatán, led in the name of a Talking Cross, that resisted Mexico for half a century during the Caste War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">Caste</a> War broke out in Yucatán in 1847. When Maya rebels were driven back into the eastern forests, a cross appeared in 1850 at a cenote near the settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felipe-carrillo-puerto-quintana-roo">Chan Santa Cruz</a> and began to speak, guiding them in war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-barrera">José María Barrera</a>, a rebel leader, promoted the cult, and the town became the rebels' sacred capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Cruzob, people of the cross, combined Maya and Catholic belief in a military theocracy. The cross's orders were conveyed through officials such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatich">Tatich</a>, or Patron of the Cross, and the Interpreter, and the population was organised in military companies that guarded the shrine. The state traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and captured goods for guns and powder with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-honduras">British Honduras</a>, which helped it survive.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> agreed a border treaty with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1893 and arms supplies dwindled, Mexican troops under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ignacio-bravo">Ignacio Bravo</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felipe-carrillo-puerto-quintana-roo">Chan Santa Cruz</a> in 1901. The town was renamed and is now Felipe Carrillo Puerto. Some Cruzob communities held out until the 1930s and still keep the cross's shrines.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chan-santa-cruz.jpg" alt="Chan Santa Cruz" width="320" height="181"><figcaption>Image: Dekodrak · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Chan_Santa_Cruz.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1850 CE – 1901 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chan Santa Cruz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>José María Barrera, Venancio Puc</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Cult of the Talking Cross (Maya Christianity)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yucatec Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cruzob, Chan Santa Cruz Maya state</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1876&amp;focus=chan-santa-cruz" aria-label="Chan Santa Cruz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2293 1460 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chan Santa Cruz lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2613 1716L2321 1717"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2321 1717h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2613 1716h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2613 1716h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chan Santa Cruz. <a href="/atlas?year=1876&amp;focus=chan-santa-cruz">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

